; Relations found in raw text, and how they relate (Capitan, 2026-11-08: "we do not have to chew its food so it eats:
; it has to learn to eat by itself and distill its own mechanisms, that reasoning emerges; we will not always be there
; to tell it how to learn"). Nobody asks it anything and nobody cuts its text: it reads the house's "pares" (the book's
; buildings, as sentido.jsm writes them) and
;   - the half: the words said most that together make half of what the book says (as in memoria_pares); the rest are
;     the words a relation can hold.
;   - the relations: every form "_ <one to three words> _" whose two places hold words not of the half (and not the
;     same word) is said by the pair it holds. A form said by two pairs is a relation (what one says is a memory, what
;     two say is a rule).
;   - how relations relate: two relations that hold the same pair, the same way ("abraham fue padre de isaac", "isaac,
;     padre de jacob") or the other way round ("jacob, hijo de isaac"), share it. Against the null: each relation's
;     pairs crossed among themselves (every pair's second word given to the pair before it), so what the two hold
;     alike is only what words they use, not which goes with which. Two relations relate when two pairs say it and it
;     shares more than its null. (A pair is crossed with the pair one before it; with "cruce" in the house, n before:
;     another shuffle of the same world, to see how much what it finds depends on the one shuffle.)
;   - the while (Capitan, 2026-11-08: "a chained while: my idea, everything in while, applied as a mechanism"), by
;     reasoning and not by chance (Capitan: "I do not want statistics, I want reasoning"): a join is a hypothesis, "S
;     is R" or "S is R the other way round". What the book says as the hypothesis deduces confirms it; what it says
;     the other way round contradicts it. Two confirmations and no contradiction, and each relation joins the one that
;     confirms it most, if that one chooses it too; the joined ones are one relation from then on (their pairs
;     together, turned when they are the other way round), and everything is deduced again with what was joined. Only
;     relations that assert something join: one that says a pair both ways ("a de b" and "b de a") does not commit
;     to a side, nothing can refute it, and it is glue, not a relation. And what each relation is refutes too: when
;     every A it says has one B, shown by two (each child one father), a join that gives an A two B's contradicts it.
;     And a hypothesis must explain what it says of the same people: of what one relation says of two words the other
;     knows (its A on the other's A side, its B on its B side), what the other says too is explained, what it does not
;     is left unexplained. Each must explain more of the other's people than it leaves unexplained; if not, the
;     hypothesis is refuted, in a coma (Capitan: "not refuted for good, in a coma: who knows if we need it some day"),
;     with what it left unexplained as its context: the people it could not account for. It wakes ("despierta:") when
;     something like what refuted it comes back (Capitan: "if there is a context like the one that made them, they may
;     wake; if something starts as an absurd, the answer may be there"): when a join brings pairs about those people
;     (news about them); then it is judged again with what is known (a form of grammar that coincides in three pairs and says many more of the same
;     people does not explain them), and each relation looks for another. What is left unexplained may be one place
;     with two names ("obed" in Mateo, "jobed" in Lucas: each said the same twice, so a name, not a slip): when the
;     other names it so with the same partners on both sides, one in each telling, it is explained, and said.
;   - deducing: what is proved becomes a premise (see hl_derive): each relation that asserts something is chained with
;     itself as a new relation, which lives if a join takes it and dies if nobody uses it. While a turn joins something, there is
;     another; when nothing new is deduced without contradiction, the while ends by itself.
;   - the tanda, when the house has "tanda" (k forms, the cube's side n): instead of the while, the k forms of the
;     strongest relations of relations, each said with the three bottom pieces of the cube its pairs fall on most (and
;     with its pairs crossed, the null), for voluntad_hilos.jsm to play with.
